  "summary": "Agentic systems are moving from grounded data analysis and dataset readiness criteria toward autonomous instrument-driven experiments.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that agent usefulness depends on explicit grounding. AI-FAIR proposes metadata-first criteria and an agentic evaluation tool to judge whether ecology and biodiversity datasets are AI-ready, while LabWeaver pairs agentic data analysis with laboratory-tier memory and skills, tested on a synovial sarcoma scRNA-seq dataset. What is new in this set is the extension of such agents to physical execution. AutoDNA, described as an AI-native experimental laboratory, reports more than 11,000 autonomous steps across over 20 instrument types for DNA synthesis, sequencing and storage. The items differ in scope and maturity, with one published item and two preprints, so evidence that grounded agents can span data, knowledge and physical execution remains early and largely descriptive.",
